1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,870
Everyone is a programmer now. They type a
wish, and the machine writes the code.

2
00:00:07,270 --> 00:00:09,510
And that worries you?

3
00:00:11,270 --> 00:00:18,814
I spent years learning what happens
underneath. Memory. Pointers. Why things

4
00:00:18,814 --> 00:00:19,410
break.

5
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:32,170
Punch cards. Assembly. C. Every step was
someone trying to say what they meant more

6
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:33,170
easily.

7
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:38,240
But if it's easy, who will know how it
works when it breaks?

8
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:44,250
Someone always does. The one who wants to.

9
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:53,602
Before you, the scribe ground his own ink
for the manuscript. The weaver counted

10
00:00:53,602 --> 00:00:59,730
every thread of the shemma. They feared
the new thing too.

11
00:01:00,630 --> 00:01:08,130
And yet the craft is still here. You are
just the newest one in a very long line.

12
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:15,150
Do you remember the first time the screen
said "Hello, World" back?

13
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:18,330
I laughed out loud.

14
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:24,960
Maybe that's the point. More people will
get to feel that laugh.
